/*
# ValueControl

The base of a control that **holds a value a person sets** — a checkbox, a switch, a
choice of one, a date, a number — and so can be checked, required, refused by a service,
and gathered into a `Form`. It is a `Control` (focus, keyboard, `disabled`) with the words
a field needs, the same words a `TextInput` has:

- `required`, `validate(v)` and `refused` say what can be wrong;
- `problem` is what is wrong **now**, `valid` that nothing is, and `error` is `problem`
  once the control has been **judged** — left, `judge()`d, or submitted with its form;
- `name` is its address in its form: the key a service names it by.

It is also where **editing** is defined, once, for every value control. The person changes
what the control shows; the control asks `admit` about the change, shows the result, and
hands it to `commit`, which puts it into your state. While the person is editing, what
they're doing is theirs: what your `commit` does to your value isn't shown until the
edit ends. Any other change to your value ends the edit and is shown at once. With no
edit open, the control shows your value.

A concrete control says what its value is by setting `fieldValue` (a `Checkbox`'s is
`checked`, a `Combobox`'s its `value`), declares the `commit` that keeps it, and reports
what the person does with `edit(v)`. It reads `shown` for what to draw. Extend it for a
value control of your own:

```declare
class Rating extends ValueControl [ width = 120, height = 24,
    stars: number = 0,
    fieldValue = { stars },
    empty = { stars == 0 },
    commit(v: number) { stars = v },
    press() { edit(stars % 5 + 1) },
    TextLabel [ text = { "★".repeat(classroot.shown) + "☆".repeat(5 - classroot.shown) } ]
    ]

App [ Rating [ x = 20, y = 20, required = "Rate your stay." ] ]
```

## commit()
`commit(v)`: the person's change, to put into your state. Each control's own `commit`
keeps it in the control's value (a `Checkbox`'s writes `checked`); override it to put
it where your state lives — `checked = { app.muted }` with `commit(v: boolean) { app.muted = v }`,
or the shorthand `checked <-> app.muted`. What you do with it is yours: store it, convert
it, clamp it, or leave your state alone because what's shown isn't a value yet. An override
that keeps no state of its own and doesn't call `super.commit(v)` leaves the control unable
to change.

## commitOn
When `commit` is called: `"each"` (the default) after every change, or `"end"` once,
when the edit ends — a title saved once, not on every keystroke; a slider drag that is one
change, and one undo step.

## admit()
`admit(v)`: asked about every change before it is shown. Return what to show — usually `v`
itself, sometimes a tidied version (a code in capitals, a pasted sentence reduced to its
digits) — or `null` to refuse the change. Returning what is already shown also refuses. The
default admits everything.

## edit()
`edit(v)`: what a control's own code calls when the person changes what it shows to `v`.
It asks `admit`, shows the result and calls `commit` as `commitOn` says. Outside a
`beginEdit()` … `endEdit()` pair the edit begins and ends at once, as a press does.

## beginEdit()
Opens an edit that takes time — a drag, typing — so the `edit(v)` calls that follow belong
to one edit, and what the control shows is the person's until it ends.

## endEdit()
Ends the open edit: with `commitOn = "end"` it commits what is shown, then the control shows
your value. Leaving the control, and Return in a text field, end the edit this way; your own
code may too.

## cancelEdit()
Ends the open edit without committing what isn't committed yet, and shows your value —
what Escape does.

## editing
**Read-only.** Whether an edit is open.

## shown
**Read-only.** What the control shows: the person's edit while one is open, your value
(`fieldValue`) otherwise. A control draws from it.

A control inside another control is part of it, not a member of a form of its own: the
entry field of a `Combobox` belongs to the combobox, which is the member.

## name
The control's address in its form — the key a service names it by when it refuses a value
(`{ "field": "country", "message": "…" }`). A path is allowed (`"address.country"`). It is
independent of the control's name in the view tree; leave it empty for a control no
service names.

## required
`true`, or **the message to say**, when the control may not be left empty:
`required = "Choose the country."`. Empty is what `empty` says. With `true` the message is
"Required"; say a message of your own wherever a person will read it. Screen readers hear
the control as required either way.

## validate()
The control's own rule: given the value, return `""` when it is fine, or the sentence that
says what is wrong. It runs when `required` is met, and it is **reactive** — a rule that
reads another value (`app.form.country`) judges again when that value changes.

## refused
**Read-only.** An outside message about the value — usually a service's refusal, laid here by a
form's `refuse` or the control's own `refuse(message)`, the one way to say it. It is the control's `problem` while the
value is the one refused, and stops counting when the value changes.

## refuse()
`refuse(message)` says an outside message about the current value (see `refused`); `""`
withdraws it.

## problem
What is wrong with the value **now**, whatever has been judged: the refusal, then
`required`, then `validate`. Empty when nothing is. The fact any presentation can be built
on; a `Form` lists its members' problems.

## valid
Whether `problem` is empty.

## error
`problem` once the control is judged, else empty — the default display rule, and what a
`Field` shows under the control and a screen reader hears with it. A control that should
say what is wrong at every moment is judged from the start: `judged = true`.

## judged
Whether the control has been judged: left, `judge()`d, or submitted with its form.
Writable: set it back to false for a fresh try (a security code emptied after a decline).

## judge()
Judges the control now, as leaving it would.

## form
The form the control belongs to: the nearest `Form` above it, unless set — set it
(`form = { app.payment }`) for a control that sits outside its form's views, as in a sheet
of its own.

## fieldValue
The value the rules judge: `validate(v)` receives it, `required` asks whether it is
`empty`, and a refusal is said of it. Each concrete control sets it from its own value, and
a control of your own does too (`fieldValue = { value }`): it is what `shown` shows while the
person edits, so without it the control shows nothing of its value.

## empty
Whether `fieldValue` counts as empty for `required`: `blank(fieldValue)` by default. A
control whose empty is something else says so (a rating of 0).

## blank()
`blank(v)`: null, `""`, `false` or an empty list.

## onRetire
A control that goes leaves its form and its `Field`. An override calls `super.onRetire()`.

## focusField()
Puts the focus where a person fixes this control's value — the control itself, or its
entry field when it has one. A form calls it to take the person to a problem.

## accessibilityLabel
The caption of the `Field` it is in, so a screen reader names the control by it; empty outside
one. A control that says its own name sets it.

## accessibilityError
Follows `error`: the control is invalid to a screen reader while it says something.

## accessibilityRequired
Follows `required`.

## _refusedValue
**Internal.** the value `refused` was said of.

## _refusing
**Internal.** the refusal still counts (the value is the one refused).

## _missing
**Internal.** required and empty.

## _requiredSays
**Internal.** the message a missing value says.

## _form
**Internal.** a control's parts belong to it, not to a form of their own.

## _field
**Internal.** the same for a `Field` around the control.

## _inField
**Internal.** the `Field` this control is in, if any.

## _draft
**Internal.** what the open edit shows.

## _spanning
**Internal.** an edit is open across several changes (`beginEdit`).

## _answering
**Internal.** this control's own `commit` has run and its answer is still to be seen: the next
change of the value is that answer, not a change from outside.

## _take()
**Internal.** shows an admitted change and commits it as `commitOn` says.

## _commitNow()
**Internal.** calls `commit`, marking its settle as the control's own.
*/

include [ "control.declare" ]

class ValueControl extends Control [
    name: string = "",
    required: boolean | string = false,
    judged: boolean = false,

    // what the rules judge, set by each concrete control from its own value

    fieldValue: any = null,
    empty: boolean = { this.blank(this.shown) },
    blank(v: any) -> boolean {
        if (v == null || v === "" || v === false) return true
        return Array.isArray(v) && v.length == 0
        },

    // a refusal is said OF a value: it counts while the value is that one

    refused: string = "",
    _refusedValue: any = null,

    validate(v: any) -> string { return "" },

    // editing: what the person is doing is theirs until the edit ends

    commitOn: "each" | "end" = "each",
    editing: boolean = false,
    _draft: any = null,
    _spanning: boolean = false,
    _answering: boolean = false,
    shown: any = { this.editing ? this._draft : this.fieldValue },

    admit(v: any) -> any { return v },
    commit(v: any) { },
    edit(v: any) {
        const a = admit(v)
        // null refuses a change; a change *to* null (clearing a choice) is not refused by it
        if ((a === null && v !== null) || (a === shown && v !== shown)) return
        _take(a)
        },
    beginEdit() {
        if (editing) return
        _spanning = true
        _draft = fieldValue
        editing = true
        },
    endEdit() {
        if (!editing) return
        const d = _draft
        _spanning = false
        editing = false
        if (commitOn == "end") _commitNow(d)
        },
    cancelEdit() {
        _spanning = false
        editing = false
        },
    _take(v: any) {
        if (!_spanning) { _commitNow(v); return }
        _draft = v
        if (commitOn != "end") _commitNow(v)
        },

    // the commit's own answer is the change the watcher below sees next, in this
    // settle's close (after its steps); a commit that changed nothing leaves none,
    // so the flag is let go here rather than left to swallow an outside change

    _commitNow(v: any) {
        const before = fieldValue
        _answering = true
        commit(v)
        afterSettle(() => { if (this.fieldValue === before) this._answering = false })
        },

    _refusing: boolean = { this.refused != "" && this.shown === this._refusedValue },
    _missing: boolean = { this.required !== false && this.required !== "" && this.empty },
    _requiredSays: string = { typeof this.required == "string" ? this.required : "Required" },
    problem: string = { this._refusing ? this.refused : this._missing ? this._requiredSays : this.validate(this.shown) },
    valid: boolean = { this.problem == "" },
    error: string = { this.judged ? this.problem : "" },

    // the form above, unless set; what is inside this control is part of it,
    // so it provides "no form" (and "no field") to its own parts

    form: any = { provided("_form", null) },
    _form: any = null,
    _field: any = null,
    _inField: any = { provided("_field", null) },

    accessibilityLabel = { provided("fieldLabel", "") },
    accessibilityError = { this.error },
    accessibilityRequired = { this.required !== false && this.required !== "" },

    refuse(message: string) {
        _refusedValue = shown
        refused = message
        },
    judge() { judged = true },
    focusField() { Focus.focus(this) },

    // a control that goes leaves its form and its field (an override calls super.onRetire())

    onRetire() { _member.retire() },

    // Membership and judging on leave are kept by this member, not by the
    // control's own handlers, so a subclass's `onInit` or `onBlur` cannot
    // drop them: it joins the form the control is in, reports the control to
    // the Field around it, and judges the control when the focus leaves it.

    _member: Node [
        f: any = { classroot.form },
        fld: any = { classroot._inField },
        here: boolean = { classroot.focused },
        v: any = { classroot.fieldValue },
        _joined: any = null,
        _held: any = null,
        trackChanges = ["f", "fld", "here", "v"],
        sync() {
            if (_joined !== f) {
                if (_joined != null) _joined._leave(classroot)
                _joined = f
                if (f != null) f._join(classroot)
                }
            if (_held !== fld) {
                if (_held != null) _held._release(classroot)
                _held = fld
                if (fld != null) fld._hold(classroot)
                }
            },
        onInit() { sync() },
        onChange(e: ChangeEvent) {
            sync()
            // the value changed from outside while an edit was open: the edit ends,
            // and the control shows the new value (its own commit's answers wait)
            if (e.changed.some((c) => c.name == "v")) {
                if (classroot._answering) classroot._answering = false
                else if (classroot.editing) classroot.cancelEdit()
                }
            // leaving ends the edit and judges — but not for the control's own popup
            // (a date picker's calendar), which the focus visits while choosing
            if (e.changed.some((c) => c.name == "here") && !here
                && classroot.accessibilityExpanded !== true) {
                classroot.endEdit()
                classroot.judged = true
                }
            },
        retire() {
            if (_joined != null) _joined._leave(classroot)
            if (_held != null) _held._release(classroot)
            _joined = null
            _held = null
            }
        ]
    ]
